                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/8394: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-03-14T17:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 14,
                                1927—5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received March 14—9:35
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>214. My 207, March 10, 6 p.m. Following from American consul general at Hankow,
                    March 12, 6 p.m.: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Lodged written protest with Ch’en through Commissioner of Foreign
                            Affairs yesterday and saw Ch’en today and protested activities of the
                            mob at Wuhu. Ch’en said that he had already sent a telegram to the
                            authorities, Wuhu directing that strict order be maintained and that no
                            foreigners or their property should be molested. He said that he would
                            send another telegram especially urging protection of life and property
                            Americans.</p>
                        <p>I am now lodging protest against further depredations just reported by
                            Price at Foochow.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
